.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt DSA_GET0_PQG 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm DSA_get0_pqg ,
.Nm DSA_set0_pqg ,
.Nm DSA_get0_key ,
.Nm DSA_set0_key ,
.Nm DSA_get0_p ,
.Nm DSA_get0_q ,
.Nm DSA_get0_g ,
.Nm DSA_get0_pub_key ,
.Nm DSA_get0_priv_key ,
.Nm DSA_clear_flags ,
.Nm DSA_test_flags ,
.Nm DSA_set_flags ,
.Nm DSA_get0_engine
.Nd Routines for getting and setting data in a DSA object
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/dsa.h
The following functions have been deprecated since OpenSSL 3.0, and can
be hidden entirely by defining
.Ar OPENSSL_API_COMPAT
with a suitable version value, see
.Xr openssl_user_macros 7 :
.Ft void
.Fo DSA_get0_pqg
.Fa "const DSA *d"
.Fa "const BIGNUM **p"
.Fa "const BIGNUM **q"
.Fa "const BIGNUM **g"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo DSA_set0_pqg
.Fa "DSA *d"
.Fa "BIGNUM *p"
.Fa "BIGNUM *q"
.Fa "BIGNUM *g"
.Fc
.Ft void
.Fo DSA_get0_key
.Fa "const DSA *d"
.Fa "const BIGNUM **pub_key"
.Fa "const BIGNUM **priv_key"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo DSA_set0_key
.Fa "DSA *d"
.Fa "BIGNUM *pub_key"
.Fa "BIGNUM *priv_key"
.Fc
.Ft const BIGNUM *
.Fo DSA_get0_p
.Fa "const DSA *d"
.Fc
.Ft const BIGNUM *
.Fo DSA_get0_q
.Fa "const DSA *d"
.Fc
.Ft const BIGNUM *
.Fo DSA_get0_g
.Fa "const DSA *d"
.Fc
.Ft const BIGNUM *
.Fo DSA_get0_pub_key
.Fa "const DSA *d"
.Fc
.Ft const BIGNUM *
.Fo DSA_get0_priv_key
.Fa "const DSA *d"
.Fc
.Ft void
.Fo DSA_clear_flags
.Fa "DSA *d"
.Fa "int flags"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo DSA_test_flags
.Fa "const DSA *d"
.Fa "int flags"
.Fc
.Ft void
.Fo DSA_set_flags
.Fa "DSA *d"
.Fa "int flags"
.Fc
.Ft ENGINE *
.Fo DSA_get0_engine
.Fa "DSA *d"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
All of the functions described on this page are deprecated.
Applications should instead use
.Xr EVP_PKEY_get_bn_param 3 .
.Pp
A DSA object contains the parameters
.Fa p ,
.Fa q
and
.Fa g .
It also contains a public key
.Pf ( Fa pub_key )
and (optionally) a private key
.Pf ( Fa priv_key Ns ).
.Pp
The
.Fa p ,
.Fa q
and
.Fa g
parameters can be obtained by calling
.Fn DSA_get0_pqg .
If the parameters have not yet been set then
.Sy *p ,
.Sy *q
and
.Sy *g
will be set to NULL. Otherwise they are set to pointers to their
respective values.
These point directly to the internal representations of the values and
therefore should not be freed directly.
.Pp
The
.Fa p ,
.Fa q
and
.Fa g
values can be set by calling
.Fn DSA_set0_pqg
and passing the new values for
.Fa p ,
.Fa q
and
.Fa g
as parameters to the function.
Calling this function transfers the memory management of the values to
the DSA object, and therefore the values that have been passed in should
not be freed directly after this function has been called.
.Pp
To get the public and private key values use the
.Fn DSA_get0_key
function.
A pointer to the public key will be stored in
.Sy *pub_key ,
and a pointer to the private key will be stored in
.Sy *priv_key .
Either may be NULL if they have not been set yet, although if the
private key has been set then the public key must be.
The values point to the internal representation of the public key and
private key values.
This memory should not be freed directly.
.Pp
The public and private key values can be set using
.Fn DSA_set0_key .
The public key must be non-NULL the first time this function is called
on a given DSA object.
The private key may be NULL. On subsequent calls, either may be NULL,
which means the corresponding DSA field is left untouched.
As for
.Fn DSA_set0_pqg
this function transfers the memory management of the key values to the
DSA object, and therefore they should not be freed directly after this
function has been called.
.Pp
Any of the values
.Fa p ,
.Fa q ,
.Fa g ,
.Fa priv_key ,
and
.Fa pub_key
can also be retrieved separately by the corresponding function
.Fn DSA_get0_p ,
.Fn DSA_get0_q ,
.Fn DSA_get0_g ,
.Fn DSA_get0_priv_key ,
and
.Fn DSA_get0_pub_key ,
respectively.
.Pp
.Fn DSA_set_flags
sets the flags in the
.Fa flags
parameter on the DSA object.
Multiple flags can be passed in one go (bitwise ORed together).
Any flags that are already set are left set.
.Fn DSA_test_flags
tests to see whether the flags passed in the
.Fa flags
parameter are currently set in the DSA object.
Multiple flags can be tested in one go.
All flags that are currently set are returned, or zero if none of the
flags are set.
.Fn DSA_clear_flags
clears the specified flags within the DSA object.
.Pp
.Fn DSA_get0_engine
returns a handle to the ENGINE that has been set for this DSA object, or
NULL if no such ENGINE has been set.
.Sh NOTES
Values retrieved with
.Fn DSA_get0_key
are owned by the DSA object used in the call and may therefore
.Em not
be passed to
.Fn DSA_set0_key .
If needed, duplicate the received value using
.Xr BN_dup 3
and pass the duplicate.
The same applies to
.Fn DSA_get0_pqg
and
.Fn DSA_set0_pqg .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn DSA_set0_pqg
and
.Fn DSA_set0_key
return 1 on success or 0 on failure.
.Pp
.Fn DSA_test_flags
returns the current state of the flags in the DSA object.
.Pp
.Fn DSA_get0_engine
returns the ENGINE set for the DSA object or NULL if no ENGINE has been
set.
